 Cached JPEG data may occupy at most 12 GiB. Before admitting a completed sheet, evict enough existing sheets to remain within that bound. Temporary render output is kept separately and removed on completion or failure. Eviction never deletes source footage or changes job-ticket semantics.
 
-The eviction policy is still open. Candidates are LRU, frequency-based eviction with aging, and 2Q. LRU has little bookkeeping and follows recent work quickly but is vulnerable to sequential scans. Aged frequency counts protect repeatedly popular clips but add counter maintenance and can lag a project switch. 2Q separates first-use entries from reused entries, resisting scans at the cost of extra queues and a queue-size parameter. None changes the external interface, queue concurrency, or disk budget.
+The worker uses 2Q eviction to protect reused comparison sheets during sequential screenings. On first access a sheet enters Q1, a FIFO queue of first-use entries. A second access promotes the entry to Q2, a FIFO queue of reused entries. Eviction removes from Q1 first; when Q1 is empty, eviction removes from Q2. Q1 and Q2 together are bounded by the 12 GiB limit, with a size split tuned for the workload. Replacement metadata, queue membership, and promotion counters are private to the preview worker. The preview API and render queue remain unchanged.
+
+## Decisions & Alternatives
+
+| Decision | Chosen | Alternatives Considered | Rationale |
+|----------|--------|------------------------|-----------|
+| Cache eviction policy | 2Q with first-use and reused queues | LRU; frequency-based eviction with aging | Protects repeatedly accessed comparison sheets from one-off screening scans. Extra bookkeeping for two queues and a size split is accepted to avoid evicting useful comparison sheets during big screenings. |
+
+## Open Questions & Future Decisions
+
+### Resolved
+1. ✅ Eviction policy selected as 2Q after evaluating bookkeeping cost vs retention of comparison sheets.
+
+### Deferred
+1. Queue size split between Q1 and Q2 to be tuned based on observed hit rates in pilot.
